{
  "trace_id": "hybrid::q09",
  "architecture": "hybrid",
  "question": "What is few-shot prompting, and what tradeoff does it create specifically in a RAG prompt?",
  "answer": "Few\u2011shot prompting is the technique of putting a handful of complete input\u2011output demonstrations straight into the prompt so the model can learn the desired format and behavior from those examples rather than from abstract instructions alone\u202f[ few-shot-prompting-for-grounded-answers::0 ].  \n\nIn a retrieval\u2011augmented generation (RAG) setting this creates a concrete trade\u2011off: every example consumes tokens that could otherwise be used for the retrieved evidence, the system instructions, or the model\u2019s answer. Because the context window is limited, adding multiple or verbose few\u2011shot examples reduces the space available for the retrieved chunks, leaving less room for the evidence the model needs to ground its response\u202f[ few-shot-prompting-for-grounded-answers::1 ].",
